== 2. Live vs replay: the same heads decoded again in the frame loop [MEASURED]
per frame after predict_sync (call A, live): B immediately; C after time.sleep(7.5 ms); D after the six head tensors are rewritten on the main thread; E after a second dispatch and readback of the same staged input (identical heads, asserted)
  user_calib stills, 4 cycles, 100 frames, 5.44 detections/frame: A 0.2367, B 0.1968, C 0.2385, D 0.1908, E 0.2364 ms (medians)
  live camera, 120 frames, 4.93 detections/frame:                 A 0.2869, B 0.2047, C 0.2462, D 0.1928, E 0.2327 ms
-> heads freshly written by the readback threads cost ~0.04 ms (E - D), a 7.5 ms sleep ~0.04 ms (C - B), and even B, with nothing in between, is about twice the replay loop's 0.09-0.10 ms (DERIVED)

== 3. Fixed host work in the frame loop: zlib.crc32 over private buffers [MEASURED]
buffers calibrated in a tight loop: small 55,163 B at 0.0100 ms, large 501,507 B at 0.0896 ms; no decode in these frames
                                   stills (100 frames)        camera (120 frames)
  first thing after the NPU returns small 0.0193 / large 0.1629  small 0.0201 / large 0.1645 ms
  after the prune and gather        small 0.0186 / large 0.1622  small 0.0187 / large 0.1628 ms
  after a 7.5 ms sleep              small 0.0200 / large 0.1627  small 0.0199 / large 0.1638 ms
  tight loop again at the end: small 0.0101, large 0.0900 ms
  cold prune (flatnonzero of cls_max per head) 0.0521 / 0.0503 ms; cold ndarray.take of the surviving anchors' 144 bytes each: 0.0246 ms (38 candidates, 5,472 B) / 0.0154 ms (22 candidates, 3,168 B)
the same crc32 workloads without any NPU object in the process (medians, x = ratio to the tight loop):
  after sleep 0.5 / 2 / 7.5 / 33 ms: small x1.71 / x1.77 / x1.83 / x1.91, large x1.63 / x1.64 / x1.65 / x1.69
  after a busy spin of the same lengths: small x1.04 / x1.05 / x1.07 / x1.16, large x1.00 / x1.00 / x1.00 / x1.02
  after an NPU dispatch awaited by run.wait(): small x1.93, large x1.80; by polling run.state(): x1.88, x1.82; run.wait() with OMP_WAIT_POLICY=PASSIVE: x1.68, x1.63
-> host work after the thread blocks runs 1.6-1.9x slower whatever blocked it, and the factor multiplies the work rather than adding a fixed cost; spinning instead of sleeping removes it, polling the NPU run does not (DERIVED)
-> so a decode that does less work loses proportionally less to it. The earlier attribution of the live decode gap to the first read of 672 KB of class logits (graph_engine_latency_phoenix_20260914T0411Z.log section 7) covers at most the E - D part: with the class maxima native, the live numpy decode was still 0.24-0.29 ms (DERIVED)

== 4. What a native decode must reproduce to return identical detections [MEASURED]
np.exp on float32 gives an element the same bits whatever the layout: one element at a time, 15 sub-slice offsets, a 256x256 grid, the negated transposed (n, 80) view the decoder builds, in place on (1, 16, 4, n); on numpy 2.5.3 and on numpy 1.26.4: PASS
sum(axis=1) over a C-contiguous (1, 16, 4, n) float32 array equals accumulation in index order ((e0 + e1) + e2) ... for 200 random shapes on both numpy versions: PASS; float32 array op Python float stays float32 on both
OpenCV 5.0.0 and 4.11.0 sources (modules/dnn/src/nms.cpp, nms.inl.hpp, modules/core/include/opencv2/core/types.hpp): NMSBoxesBatchedImpl, NMSFast_, GetMaxScoreIndex, rectOverlap, Rect_ operator&= and jaccardDistance are identical (nms.cpp differs by one #include)
  max_coord over x1, y1, x1 + w, y1 + h in double starting at 0; offset class_id * (max_coord + 1); scores strictly above score_threshold, std::stable_sort descending; greedy keep when (float)overlap <= nms_threshold with overlap = 1.f - (float)(1 - inter / (Aa + Ab - inter)); eta 1, top_k 0
build: preprocess_simd.c is compiled with /fp:fast, which may reassociate or contract float arithmetic, so the decode is its own library built with cl /O2 /fp:precise (no /arch, so no FMA) and #pragma fp_contract(off); cl /W4 reports no warnings

== 5. Design iterations [MEASURED]
version 1 (scalar C; exp and sigmoid from numpy-filled tables; OpenCV NMS in C):
  parity: stress 2,000 trials on each env, 0 mismatches; 156 recorded frames, 0 mismatches
  replay hot medians native / numpy: camera 0.0215 / 0.0917, JPEGs 0.0246 / 0.1028, bus 0.0259 / 0.1088 ms
  live, paths alternating per frame: stills native 0.0572 / numpy 0.2469 ms; camera (4.48 detections/frame) native 0.0649 / numpy 0.2804 ms
  hot profile on 40 recorded camera frames: postprocess 21.1 us = C work 15.1 + ctypes arguments 1.0 + binding check and tolist 2.2 + detection objects 2.8; a C call with no survivors 3.6 us, so ~12.6 us of per-candidate work
version 2 (final): integer threshold (for an integer m, m > q_t exactly when m > floor(q_t)) with one SSE2 movemask scan for survivors; the class score is the sigmoid table at the class maximum and the class is the first whose logit reaches the lowest logit with the same float32 sigmoid (numpy's argmax, saturation included); the 16 DFL divisions and weightings four at a time in SSE2 with the sums kept in index order
  hot profile: postprocess 8.0 us = C work 2.6 + ctypes arguments 1.0 + binding check and tolist 1.5 + detection objects 2.9; a C call with no survivors 1.4 us
prefetching the surviving anchors' box logits before decoding them: stills native 0.0377 ms with, 0.0397 ms without (two runs a minute apart); the camera comparison is void (0.49 detections/frame in the prefetch run) -> no measurable gain, removed

